          "param": "rounding"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput = Fmax. To hit 50 MSPS it needs Fmax >= 50 MHz. A full pipeline of N stages is the most area/power-hungry variant, so it is the 'high accuracy / high power' anchor of the front. I keep the full n_iter/data_width sweep so Optuna can find the minimum-N, minimum-W point that still clears max_abs_err <= 2^-12, and the rounding/guard trade-offs that trade a few accuracy bits against area/power. This maps the expensive end of the trade-off curve."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 30,
          "low": 4,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 28,
          "low": 8,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 8,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 1,
          "low": 1,
          "param": "rounding"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers only every m stages, so it keeps 1 result/cycle (throughput = Fmax) while cutting FF count and area versus the full pipeline. This is the most promising family for the min-power objective: fewer registers means lower power_index and smaller area, and the longer combinational path per stage can still clear 50 MHz for moderate m. Sweeping m (2..8) together with n_iter/data_width lets Optuna find the sweet spot where the path is fast enough for 50 MSPS but the register count is minimised, mapping the low-power end of the front."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands throughput >= 50 MSPS and max_abs_err <= 2^-12, minimising power_index. Only the pipelined and pipelined_m families produce 1 result/cycle, so throughput equals Fmax and the throughput constraint reduces to Fmax >= 50 MHz; the iterative/unrolled_k families (not in my fleet) would need Fmax >= 50*k and are excluded anyway. I split the 100-eval budget evenly: pipelined_m is the likely winner for min power (fewer registers, same 1/cycle throughput) so it anchors the low-power end, while pipelined anchors the high-accuracy/high-power end. Both sweep the full accuracy-relevant ranges (n_iter, data_width, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding) so the merged front covers the whole accuracy-vs-power trade-off curve, which is what the hypervolume rewards. The reserved front-mapping portion of the budget will then refine these ranges over the full family extents."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The Pareto front is well-mapped and feasible. The minimum power_index on the front is 6.1 (pipelined, data_width=26, n_iter=17), which satisfies the throughput constraint (98.8 MSPS >= 50) and the accuracy constraint (16 bits > 12 bits required). The front extends to 21.9 bits accuracy, covering the trade-off space adequately. Since this is the final round, we stop and select the design with minimum power_index (6.1) as the final design."
,
